---
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
---

<Page
  title="FAQ"
  description="Short answers about Emaki: price, platforms, offline use, accounts, Figma import, motion tracking, file format, export formats and open source."
  heading="Questions and answers"
>
  <p slot="intro">Price, platforms, privacy and file formats, answered briefly. If yours isn't here, it may be on the <a href={url('/roadmap')}>roadmap</a>.</p>

  <div class="faq">
    <section>
      <h2>Is it free? For good?</h2>
      <p class="short">Yes, for good</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>
        Emaki is free to download and use, with no paid tier. The code will be open source under the MIT and
        Apache 2.0 licences, so it stays free even if the project changes hands.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Which platforms does it run on?</h2>
      <p class="short">Linux now; macOS and Windows later</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>
        Linux now, as an AppImage that runs on most 64-bit distributions. macOS and Windows builds are planned for
        later, once they've been tested on real machines.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Does it work offline?</h2>
      <p class="short">Yes, apart from Google Fonts and Figma links</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>
        Designing, animating, previewing and exporting all work without a network. Emaki only goes online when you
        ask it to: when you open the Google Fonts tab in the font picker (and download a font from it), or when you
        import a frame from Figma with a link. A few optional tools download a helper the first time you choose to
        use them; after that it works offline on your computer, and your pictures never leave it.
        <a href={url('/privacy')}>More on privacy</a>
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Do I need an account?</h2>
      <p class="short">No</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>There is nothing to sign up for or sign in to, and no tracking.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Can I import from Figma?</h2>
      <p class="short">Yes, in two ways</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>The plugin, or a link:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The Emaki plugin for Figma.</strong> Select frames or layers, click <em>Copy for Emaki</em>, then
          press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> in Emaki. It works through your clipboard, with no token and no
          network. For now it installs as a development plugin in the Figma desktop app.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A link to a frame.</strong> Paste a frame's link and a Figma personal access token, and Emaki fetches
          it through Figma's API.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Either way the layers arrive as ordinary Emaki layers: frames with auto layout, shapes, text, vectors and
        images, which you can edit and animate like any other.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is motion tracking?</h2>
      <p class="short">Pinning a layer to something moving in a video</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>
        It follows a point in a video from frame to frame, so a layer can stick to it. Right-click a layer above a
        video and pin it to one point (it moves with it) or two points (it also scales and turns). Where the tracker is
        unsure, the frame is marked; drag the marker to fix it and tracking picks up from there. Tracking a screen's
        four corners puts a design onto a moving screen.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What file format does it use?</h2>
      <p class="short">A plain .emaki folder</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>
        A project is a <code>.emaki</code> folder: a <code>project.json</code> file with an <code>assets</code> folder
        of its media beside it. You can back it up, sync it or zip it like any folder. Projects from before 0.9.2,
        saved as <code>.motion</code>, still open and keep their names.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What can it export?</h2>
      <p class="short">Six formats, sound included</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>
        MP4 (H.264), WebM, ProRes 4444 with transparency, GIF, Lottie, and PNG as a single frame or a sequence. You can
        export one artboard or all of them at once. Sound from audio and video layers is mixed into MP4, WebM and
        ProRes. FFmpeg comes inside the AppImage, so every format works out of the box.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When will the code be public?</h2>
      <p class="short">Soon</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>There's no date yet. This site will link to it as soon as it's out.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How can I help?</h2>
      <p class="short">Once the code is public</p>
      <div class="long">
      <p>
        Once the code is public, bug reports, ideas and pull requests are all welcome. See
        <a href={url('/contributing')}>Contributing</a> for how to build it and how changes are made.
      </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Page>

<style>
  /* Each answer in a few words first, big enough to scan; the detail beside it. */
  .faq {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
  }
  .faq section {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px 64px;
    padding-block: 28px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .faq section {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr);
      grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
    }
    .long {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1 / span 2;
    }
  }
  .faq h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .short {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 2.4vw, 1.875rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--text);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .long p,
  .long li {
    color: var(--text-dim);
  }
  .long ul {
    padding-left: 20px;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  .long li + li {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .long strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .long em {
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .long > :last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
